# gnome-shell-mobile boosts the focused app's tasks with
# sched_setattr(SCHED_FLAG_UTIL_CLAMP_MIN) so schedutil raises the CPU
# frequency the moment input lands, instead of a reactive ramp several
# frames later. Without UCLAMP_TASK the kernel rejects that call and the
# shell logs "Couldn't set min utilization clamping ... sched_setattr
# failed" for every focused window (measured on device 2026-08-25).
# Per-task clamping alone is enough for that path; the cgroup-level
# UCLAMP_TASK_GROUP is deliberately left out because it depends on
# CGROUP_SCHED, and no cpu controller is compiled in (see CGROUPS below).
CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK=y
CONFIG_CGROUPS=y
# CGROUPS on its own ships an empty cgroup.controllers: no controller is
# compiled in, so systemd can account and limit nothing per unit and
# systemd-oomd is permanently ConditionPathExists-skipped. PSI is oomd's input
# (/proc/pressure/* plus per-cgroup {cpu,memory,io}.pressure) and MEMCG is the
# controller it acts on; neither is useful without the other. MEMCG also
# selects VM_EVENT_COUNTERS, which is why the "is not set" line for it is gone
# below -- kconfig would have overridden it silently.
CONFIG_PSI=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
# Namespaces are a systemd prerequisite, and CONFIG_EXPERT above turns them
# off by default: NAMESPACES is "default !EXPERT" (init/Kconfig:1403), and
# USER_NS is "default n" even under it (:1439). Without them systemd's
# PrivateUsers=yes fails at exec with 217/USER, which on device killed
# upower, colord and power-profiles-daemon on every boot -- i.e. no battery
# or AC state reached the desktop at all. UTS/IPC/PID/NET/TIME_NS are
# "default y" once NAMESPACES is on, so these two lines cover the set.
CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y
CONFIG_USER_NS=y
